WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:06.290
I’m sure we’re all familiar with M&amp;Ms,
skittles, snickers, Twix, and Milky Way bars.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.660
Those are some of the most famous candy and
chocolate brands in the world and they’re

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.089
all owned by a company called Mars inc.

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:17.100
What you may not be familiar with though is
the people behind Mars.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.439
Mars Inc is actually one of the largest privately-owned
companies in the world and the company is

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:24.930
still owned completely by the founding family.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:29.140
Today, the family occupies 6 spots on the
billionaire’s list with all of them being

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.830
deca billionaires.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:37.200
This adds up to a total of $140.8 billion
which makes them the richest private family

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.200
in the world.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.680
So, here’s the story of the real like Willy
Wonkas.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.550
Taking a look back, it all started with Franklin
Clarence Mars who was born on September 24,

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.280
1883, in Hancock Minnesota.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.630
At a young age, Frank caught polio, and in
an effort to make him feel better and have

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:00.600
a bit of fun, his mother Alva decided to make
some hand-dipped chocolate candy.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.660
This was Frank’s first introduction to chocolate
making and he found the process quite intriguing.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.110
As he grew up, Frank continued to learn how
to make various kinds of chocolate from his

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.150
mother including fudge and peanut brittle.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.490
And as soon as he turned 19, Frank began a
small home business selling molasses chips

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.580
and other types of candies to local stores.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.560
At the time, chocolate was a rather new invention.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.220
People around the world had been enjoying
sweet treats since the Egyptians.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.590
But the use of cocoa beans to make chocolate
wasn’t a thing until the late 1700s.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:35.790
Originally, chocolate was primarily consumed
as a drink by the rich, and it wasn’t until

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.510
1847 that Joseph Fry created the first chocolate
bar.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:42.420
So, it’s quite surprising that Frank’s
mother was familiar enough with chocolate

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.729
that she was able to make her own.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:49.440
Anyway, going back to Frank, he got married
quite young to a woman named Ethel G Kissack

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.090
who was a school teacher.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:56.210
They were married in 1902 when Frank was just
19 years old, and they had a son named Forrest

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.930
Mars in 1904.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.640
Unfortunately, the two would divorce soon
after, but what’s funny is that Frank would

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.720
go ahead and marry another woman named Ethel
in 1910.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.319
One year later, Frank and Ethel Healy would
start the Mars Candy Company together in Tacoma

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:12.560
Washington.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.300
The company specialized in wholesaling fresh
candy, but they weren’t the only company

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.060
in the area targeting this market.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.850
A company called Brown &amp; Haley came around
just a few years later, and they would prove

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.290
to be a lot more successful.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:29.600
Frank and Ethel had a daughter named Patricia
in 1914 and they tried to keep up the Mars

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.270
Candy Company as long as they could.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:37.590
But eventually, they threw in the towel and
moved to Minneapolis, Minnesota in 1920.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:42.100
In Minneapolis, the duo started a new chocolate
company called Mars O Bar that manufactured

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.330
chocolate bars.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:48.140
In 1923, Frank introduced a new chocolate
bar based on his son Forrest’s idea called

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.770
the Milky Way bar.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:53.930
Milky Way became a customer favorite and propelled
the business into being an up-and-coming chocolate

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:54.930
company.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:59.000
Several years later, in 1929, Frank would
move to Chicago where he would come up with

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.530
the Snickers Bar.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.480
Things were finally starting to pick, and
Frank would start to enjoy himself.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.720
In the late 1920s, Frank started construction
on a massive estate called the Milky Way Farm.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.630
The estate consisted of a 25,000 square feet
clubhouse, over 30 barns, and it even had

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.420
a horse racing track.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.980
Apparently, Frank employed 935 men to construct
the estate.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.520
But just as the estate was completed, Frank
would pass away at age 50 in 1934 due to heart

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:26.520
problems.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.569
Unfortunately, it looks like enjoying his
wealth just wasn’t in the cards.

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:34.910
With Frank dead, the responsibility of running
the company naturally fell on his kids, but

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:37.959
his son Forrest had already gone on his own
path.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:41.860
Given that Frank and his first wife Ethel
had divorced soon after Forrest’s birth,

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.230
Forrest wasn’t that close with his father.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.520
Forrest and his mother had moved to Canada
after the divorce and Forrest rarely saw his

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:49.520
father.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.300
Forrest ended up attending the University
of California Berkeley before transferring

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.910
to Yale and graduating with an industrial
engineering degree in 1928.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:01.239
After graduation, Forrest did join his father’s
company, but he didn’t stick around for

00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:02.269
long.

00:04:02.269 --> 00:04:06.800
At first, Forrest oversaw the development
of the Snickers and 3 Musketeers bars, but

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.180
soon enough, Forrest wanted to expand the
company internationally.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.740
However, Frank had no interest in expanding
internationally.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:18.709
Eventually, the argument would blow over and
Forrest would end up moving to England.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:23.289
In England, Forrest took on jobs at Nestle
and the Tobler company, but he never let go

00:04:23.289 --> 00:04:25.130
of his chocolate roots either.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:29.259
In the background, Forrest was developing
the Mars bar and Maltesers.

00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:33.370
In 1934, Forrest bought a British company
called Chappel Bros which produced canned

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:34.569
dog food.

00:04:34.569 --> 00:04:38.599
There was very little competition in the market,
so it wasn’t that hard for Forrest to dominate

00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:44.009
the dog food market, but after his father’s
death, Forrest would move back to the US.

00:04:44.009 --> 00:04:47.879
Forrest oversaw his father’s company, but
he wouldn’t take over the company for quite

00:04:47.879 --> 00:04:49.039
some time.

00:04:49.039 --> 00:04:53.629
His stepmom Ethel Healy became the president
of Mars Inc, and she was in charge of day-to-day

00:04:53.629 --> 00:04:55.020
operations.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.819
In the meantime, Forrest created his own food
business called Food Manufacturers.

00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:04.199
I think we can agree that that’s literally
the lamest name he could have given the business.

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:08.159
Despite the lame name, the company would go
on to be quite successful.

00:05:08.159 --> 00:05:12.069
Under Food Manufacturers, Forrest started
two sub-brands called Uncle Ben’s Rice and

00:05:12.069 --> 00:05:14.620
Pedigree, the famous pet food brand.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:17.940
Forrest kept coming up with new products ideas
for Mars as well.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.659
In 1940, Forrest came up with the idea for
M&amp;Ms.

00:05:21.659 --> 00:05:25.220
The idea was to create a chocolate candy that
didn’t melt your hands, but would melt in

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:26.639
your mouth.

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:30.379
Given that this was during World War 2 though,
Forrest was worried that sugar and chocolate

00:05:30.379 --> 00:05:31.610
would be rationed.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:36.159
So, Forrest formed a partnership with the
president of Hershey William Murrie.

00:05:36.159 --> 00:05:40.370
This way the two companies could share resources
if rationing was enforced.

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:44.939
This is how the name for M&amp;M came about, the
name was indicative of the partnership between

00:05:44.939 --> 00:05:46.419
Mars &amp; Murrie.

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:49.879
Ironically, these companies are massive rivals
today.

00:05:49.879 --> 00:05:55.509
Anyway, a few years later in 1945, Forrest’s
stepmom passed away, and this gave Forrest

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:59.550
and his step-sister Patricia the full responsibility
of running the company.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.000
In 1954, Forrest introduced Peanut M&amp;Ms.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.470
And fun fact, Forrest was actually extremely
allergic to peanuts.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.969
Forrest continued to run his father’s company
and his own company separately for another

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:15.349
10 years, but finally in 1964, 30 years after
his father’s death, Forrest merged the two

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:16.349
companies.

00:06:16.349 --> 00:06:20.600
It’s not clear what exactly happened to
Patricia’s stake in the business or if she

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.159
even had one.

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:27.659
Patricia died just one year later in 1965
and it doesn’t look like she had any kids,

00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:32.490
so maybe Forrest inherited her stake after
her death or maybe he bought out her stake

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.129
while she was still alive.

00:06:34.129 --> 00:06:38.129
Whatever the case though, Forrest was left
with the entire business, and given Forrest’s

00:06:38.129 --> 00:06:42.809
massive influence in the company starting
with the Milky Way bar way back in 1923, I

00:06:42.809 --> 00:06:44.770
think that’s only fitting.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:48.749
Over the next decade, Forrest continued to
run the company with a tight fist.

00:06:48.749 --> 00:06:53.159
Forrest was notorious for having an extreme
temper and he highly valued punctuality and

00:06:53.159 --> 00:06:54.629
performance.

00:06:54.629 --> 00:06:59.220
Forrest incentivized these traits by offering
a 10% bonus to all Mars employees.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:02.729
If the company was hitting performance metrics
and you were on time the entire week, you

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:04.319
got a 10% bonus that week.

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:09.119
But, if you were late even once, you automatically
lost the bonus regardless of whether you’re

00:07:09.119 --> 00:07:11.460
a janitor or an executive.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:16.969
Forrest officially retired in 1973 at age
69, but he had quite a bit of trouble actually

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:18.309
letting go.

00:07:18.309 --> 00:07:22.960
He left the company with his three children,
Forrest Jr, John Mars, and Jacqueline Mars.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.629
Apparently, he would call his kids all the
time to nitpick at things that they weren’t

00:07:26.629 --> 00:07:28.020
doing quite right.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:31.929
But eventually, Forest Sr lightened up on
his kids after he founded a new chocolate

00:07:31.929 --> 00:07:33.339
company to run.

00:07:33.339 --> 00:07:38.039
In 1981, Forest Sr moved to Henderson, Nevada,
and founded Ethel M Chocolates.

00:07:38.039 --> 00:07:43.719
He ran the company for 7 years, but in 1988,
he would sell the company to Mars Inc and

00:07:43.719 --> 00:07:44.719
he would truly retire.

00:07:44.719 --> 00:07:50.439
Forest Sr. ended up passing away in 1998 at
age 95 after living quite a stunning life.

00:07:50.439 --> 00:07:54.740
As for Mars Inc, the company only grew even
larger under the leadership of Forest’s

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:55.740
kids.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:59.509
After Forest Sr. left the company, the kids
moved the headquarters to McLean Virginia.

00:07:59.509 --> 00:08:04.369
The company is super famous for being secretive,
so we don’t know too much about their internal

00:08:04.369 --> 00:08:06.959
operations or business decisions.

00:08:06.959 --> 00:08:11.490
But one avenue of growth that we are aware
of is their public acquisitions, and the kids

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.300
haven’t been shy when it comes to acquisitions.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:18.789
They bought the chewing gum company, Wrigley
Jr., in 2008 for $23 billion.

00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:24.749
Similarly, they bought three pet food brands
from P&amp;G for $2.9 billion in 2014.

00:08:24.749 --> 00:08:27.830
They also expanded into new areas through
acquisitions.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.620
For example, in 2003, Mars acquired Aquarium
Pharmaceuticals which allowed the company

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.180
to enter the aquarium and pond markets.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:38.990
Similarly, they bought Banfield Pet Hospital
in 2007.

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:42.430
Since then, Mars has doubled down on the veterinary
market.

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:48.090
Mars went ahead and bought BluePearl Veterinary
Partners in 2015, VCA inc in 2017, and the

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.100
Linnaeus Group in 2018.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.030
All of these acquisitions have actually made
Mars the largest pet nutrition and veterinary

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:55.720
care provider in the world.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.760
Today, the kids aren’t really kids anymore
as they’re in their 80s and Forest Jr. actually

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.720
passed away in 2016.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.890
His stake in the company was even split between
his four children who all work at Mars as

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:07.910
well.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:12.810
As for John Mars, he has three kids, but we
don’t know what exactly they do and if they’re

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.630
even involved with the company.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:18.940
John himself has chosen to live an extremely
private and peaceful life in Wyoming.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:22.320
In fact, he’s kept his life so private that
when Forbes was making their billionaires

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.070
list from 2010 to 2014, they couldn’t find
a picture of him which made him the richest

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.300
person on the list without a picture.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.830
And finally, we have Jacqueline who is likely
the most public of the three.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:38.510
We don’t know too much about her three children,
but in 2013, Jacqueline was involved in an

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.950
infamous car accident near her house.

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:45.880
Jacqueline claims that she passed out while
driving but she tested negative for drugs,

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:51.030
alcohol, and medication, so it’s not clear
what exactly caused the blackout.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:56.100
But unfortunately, the accident resulted in
a person dying and a pregnant woman miscarrying.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:01.110
Jacqueline pleaded guilty and profusely apologized
for the incident.

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:05.000
She was eventually able to walk away from
the situation with a misdemeanor charge for

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.480
reckless driving.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.520
And that’s the story of the $141 billion
chocolate family.

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.370
Did you guys know about the Mars family?

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:13.580
